(1) det JK = -2  OK
    JK . adj(JK) = -2 I  OK  (adjugate exact)
(2) bracket identities:
    {X_i, X_j} = 0        : OK
    {X_i, P_j} = delta_ij : OK (9 identities)
    {P_i, P_j} = 0        : OK (3 identities, all p-coefficients)
    => Phi IS a Poisson endomorphism of (C[x,p], standard bracket).
(3) det M = det(adj^T)/(-2)^3 = 4/(-8) = -1/2 ; det JPhi = det JK * det M = 1  OK
(4) Phi at the three lifted collision points:
    Phi(('0', '0', '-1/4', '0', '0', '0')) = ('-1/4', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0')
    Phi(('1', '-3/2', '13/2', '0', '0', '0')) = ('-1/4', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0')
    Phi(('-1', '3/2', '13/2', '0', '0', '0')) = ('-1/4', '0', '0', '0', '0', '0')
    all three EQUAL: Phi is NOT injective => NOT an automorphism.

CONCLUSION: Phi = cotangent lift of the Keller kernel is an explicit
unimodular Poisson endomorphism of C[x1..x3,p1..p3] that is not an
automorphism: the POISSON CONJECTURE on C^6 is FALSE, and by identity
padding the STABLE POISSON CONJECTURE is false for all C^{2n}, n >= 3.
Classical limit of the S141 Dixmier endomorphism (symbols of its 9
Weyl commutator identities).  DONE.
